  ],
  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The V-stitch square opens the fabric on the sides while keeping the corners firm — replacing treble clusters with "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "v-stitch"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "V-stitches"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " (two trebles separated by one chain into the same space) produces a noticeably lacier square than the granny construction at the same round count. The "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "corner-group"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "corner group"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " of (3tr, ch 2, 3tr) stays unchanged from the standard granny — it is only the side positions that change to V-stitches. Start from a "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "magic-ring"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "magic ring"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "blocking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Blocking"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " opens the V-stitch chain-1 spaces to their full width."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "infoPanel",
        "attrs": {
          "type": "materials"
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Yarn: DK cotton, approx 25 m per square. Hook: 4 mm. Notions: tapestry needle, scissors."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Gauge"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "10 × 10 cm in DK cotton on a 4 mm hook. The V-stitch fabric is slightly more open than a cluster granny — gauge is looser and the blocked size may be up to 0.5 cm larger than expected from the stitch count alone."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Stitches used"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Magic ring"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Chain (ch)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Treble (tr)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "V-stitch: (tr, ch 1, tr) into same sp"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Slip stitch (sl st)"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pattern"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ch 3 at the start of each round counts as the first tr throughout. Corners: (3tr, ch 2, 3tr) — note these are individual trebles, not clusters. V-stitches on sides: (tr, ch 1, tr) into each ch-1 sp from the previous round."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 1"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make a magic ring. Ch 3 (counts as first tr). 2 tr, ch 2. *(3 tr, ch 2); repeat from * 2 more times. Sl st to top of ch-3. Pull tail to close ring. (12 tr, 4 ch-2 corners)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 2"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sl st into corner ch-2 sp. Ch 3 (counts as first tr). (2 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in same corner sp. *V-stitch (tr, ch 1, tr) into each ch-2 sp on side (1 V-stitch per side at this stage). (3 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in next corner sp; repeat from * 2 more times. V-stitch in last side sp. Sl st to top of ch-3. (24 tr in corners, 4 V-stitches on sides) = (24 tr + 8 tr in V-sts, 4 ch-2 corners, 4 ch-1 V-st sps)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 3"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sl st into corner sp. Ch 3 (counts as first tr). (2 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in corner sp. *V-stitch into each ch-1 sp of each side V-stitch (2 V-stitches per side). (3 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in next corner sp; repeat from * 2 more times. 2 V-stitches in last side sps. Sl st to top of ch-3. (24 tr in corners, 8 V-stitches on sides) = (24 tr + 16 tr in V-sts, 4 ch-2 corners, 8 ch-1 V-st sps)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Round 4"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sl st into corner sp. Ch 3 (counts as first tr). (2 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in corner sp. *V-stitch into each ch-1 sp across side (3 V-stitches per side). (3 tr, ch 2, 3 tr) in next corner sp; repeat from * 2 more times. 3 V-stitches in last side sps. Sl st to top of ch-3. Fasten off. (24 tr in corners, 12 V-stitches on sides) = (24 tr + 24 tr in V-sts, 4 ch-2 corners, 12 ch-1 V-st sps)"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chart"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "craftChart",
        "attrs": {
          "roundCount": 4
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Finishing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Weave in ends. Wet-block to 10 × 10 cm, pinning the corners and placing pins along the sides to open the V-stitch spaces. The V-stitch fabric is more open and pins more readily than a cluster square."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Joining"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Join using the flat slip-stitch method through corresponding V-stitch chain-1 spaces and corner chain-2 spaces. The V-stitch chain-1 spaces give a natural, even joining interval that aligns precisely between adjacent squares."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Care"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Machine wash at 30 degrees on a gentle cycle. Reshape while damp and dry flat. The V-stitch chain-1 spaces may close slightly after washing — a light steam-block will reopen them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What to try next"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Add a fifth round for a 12.5 cm square — follow the same pattern, adding one more V-stitch per side. Or replace the corner groups with V-stitches as well for a fully V-stitch construction, though this requires a different corner angle calculation to keep the square flat."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}